        {
         "q": "KEY CUES — What words and findings should immediately point you to neonatal tetanus, and what should point you elsewhere?",
         "lecturer": "Summary of the discriminating features drawn from the aetiology, clinical features, differential diagnosis and prognosis sections of the lecture.",
         "explain": "## The history cues — these should raise it before you examine\n\n**\"Home delivery\" · \"traditional birth attendant\" · \"delivered on the floor\"**\n\n→ Unsterile cord cutting. **Suspect tetanus.**\n\n---\n\n**\"Animal dung\" · \"ash\" · \"herbs\" · \"cow dung applied to the stump\"**\n\n→ Supplies **spores** AND creates **anaerobic** conditions. Classic.\n\n---\n\n**\"No antenatal care\" · \"mother not immunised\" · \"no tetanus toxoid\"**\n\n→ **No maternal IgG crossed the placenta.** The baby had zero protection.\n\n---\n\n**\"3rd to 14th day of life\"** — most often end of the first week\n\n→ The toxin's travel time. Fits perfectly.\n\n---\n\n## The clinical cues — the classic triad\n\n**\"Cannot suck\" + \"cries as if hungry\"**\n\n→ First symptom. **He wants to feed and physically cannot.**\n\n---\n\n**\"Cannot open the mouth\" · \"jaw locked\"**\n\n→ **Trismus** — masseter spasm. Present in **50–75%**.\n\n---\n\n**\"Fixed grin\" · \"scornful smile\" · \"grimace\"**\n\n→ **Risus sardonicus** — facial muscle spasm.\n\n---\n\n**\"Arched back\" + \"clenched fists\" + \"arms flexed and adducted\" + \"legs extended\"**\n\n→ **Opisthotonos**.\n\n---\n\n**\"Stiffens when touched / when the light is on / when there is noise\"**\n\n→ Spasms triggered by **noise, light, touch**. Prescribe the **quiet dark room**.\n\n---\n\n## The trap you must not fall into\n\n**\"The cord stump looks clean / normal / dry\"**\n\n→ **DOES NOT EXCLUDE TETANUS.**\n\n>> *C. tetani* cannot evoke an inflammatory response. A benign-looking portal of entry is the **expected** finding.\n\n---\n\n## Tetanus vs seizures — the three questions\n\n| Ask | Tetanus | Seizure |\n|---|---|---|\n| Conscious during it? | **Yes, and in pain** | **No** |\n| Stiff between episodes? | **Yes** | No — floppy, post-ictal |\n| Hands and feet involved? | **Relatively spared** | Fully involved |\n\n>> \"Marked increased tone in **central** muscles with superimposed spasms and **relative sparing of hands and feet** — suggests tetanus.\"\n\n---\n\n## Cues pointing AWAY from tetanus\n\n**\"Low blood sugar\"** → hypoglycaemia. Treat it.\n\n**\"Low serum calcium\"** → hypocalcaemic tetany.\n\n**\"Bulging fontanelle, CSF changes\"** → meningitis / encephalitis.\n\n**\"Jaw hurts to open, swelling in the throat or gum\"** → parapharyngeal, retropharyngeal or **alveolar abscess** — mechanical, not spasm.\n\n**\"Recently given metoclopramide or a phenothiazine\"** → **dystonic drug reaction**.\n\n**\"Animal bite, hydrophobia\"** → **rabies**.\n\n**\"Poisoning, adult, spasms with intact consciousness\"** → **strychnine** — blocks glycine receptors (tetanus stops glycine release).\n\n---\n\n## The other tetanus types\n\n**\"Spasm only around the wound, no CNS signs\"** → **Localised** tetanus. **Mortality very low.**\n\n**\"Chronic otitis media\" or \"head trauma\" + cranial nerve palsies, retracted eyelids, deviated gaze**\n\n→ **Cephalic** tetanus.\n\n---\n\n## The four BAD prognostic signs — know them cold\n\n1. **Onset in the first week of life**\n2. **Trismus-to-spasm interval under 48 hours**\n3. **High fever**\n4. **Tachycardia**\n\n---\n\n## Treatment triggers\n\n**\"Wildly swinging pulse and BP, sweating, hyperpyrexia\"**\n\n→ **Autonomic hyperactivity** → **magnesium sulphate**, loading 5 g (75 mg/kg), then 2.3 g/h.\n\n---\n\n**\"Patellar reflex absent\"** on magnesium\n\n→ **4 mmol/L, top of range. REDUCE THE DOSE.**\n\n---\n\n**\"Sudden respiratory deterioration during spasms\"**\n\n→ **Pneumothorax** or **laryngospasm**. Think tracheostomy.\n\n---\n\n## Two facts examiners love\n\n>> **An attack of tetanus does NOT confer immunity** — the lethal dose (2.5 ng/kg) is too small to immunise. Give the vaccine anyway.\n\n>> **Recovery is almost complete** in survivors — nerves regenerate by sprouting new terminals. But mortality is **90%**.",
         "tie": "This is your fast-recall layer.\n\nAnd remember the single most important cue of all:\n\n>> One case of neonatal tetanus means the **whole locality** is unimmunised. Treat the community, not just the baby."
        }
