         "q": "KEY CUES: What words should immediately point you to a specific chemotherapy fact?",
         "lecturer": "Key cues: tumour cells not faster but more dividing = the basis of chemo selectivity. First-order kinetics = kills a constant fraction, needs repeated cycles. Haematologic/marrow toxicity = most frequent side effect. Goldie-Coldman = resistance develops, so use combination therapy. Ovarian cancer = responds best, platinum + paclitaxel. Cervical cancer = chemoradiation (cisplatin +/- 5-FU). Vaginal cancer = chemo has no activity. BEP = ovarian germ-cell tumours. EMA-CO = medium/high risk GTD. Methotrexate = low-risk GTD. beta-hCG > 20,000 at 4 weeks / rising / choriocarcinoma / metastasis = indications for chemo in GTD. Cisplatin = nephrotoxic. Adriamycin = cardiotoxic. Bleomycin = pulmonary and skin. Methotrexate = hepatotoxic. Vincristine/bleomycin = neurotoxic. Yuzpe regimen = emergency contraception within 72 hours. Osteoporosis = most important menopausal hazard.",
         "explain": "## Group 1 — Principles\n\n>> **Not faster, just more dividing** → basis of selectivity\n\n>> **First-order kinetics** → constant fraction → **repeated cycles**\n\n>> **Marrow/haematologic toxicity** → **most frequent** side effect\n\n>> **Goldie-Coldman** → resistance → use **combination** therapy\n\n---\n\n## Group 2 — Which cancer, which drugs\n\n>> **Ovarian** → responds **best**; **platinum + paclitaxel**\n\n>> **Ovarian germ-cell** → **BEP** (bleomycin, etoposide, platinum)\n\n>> **Cervix (squamous)** → **chemoradiation** (cisplatin ± 5-FU)\n\n>> **Vagina** → chemo has **NO activity** (the exception)\n\n>> **Vulva (advanced)** → mitomycin C + 5-FU + radiotherapy\n\n---\n\n## Group 3 — GTD\n\n>> **β-hCG > 20,000 at 4 weeks / rising / choriocarcinoma / metastasis** → indications for chemo\n\n>> **Low risk** → **methotrexate**; **medium/high** → **EMA-CO**\n\n---\n\n## Group 4 — Drug-specific toxicities (exam gold)\n\n>> **Cisplatin → kidney**\n\n>> **Adriamycin (doxorubicin) → heart**\n\n>> **Bleomycin → lung (and skin)**\n\n>> **Methotrexate → liver**\n\n>> **Vincristine/bleomycin → nerves**\n\n---\n\n## Group 5 — Hormone therapy\n\n>> **Yuzpe regimen** → emergency contraception within **72 h**\n\n>> **Hot flushes → oestrogen**\n\n>> **Osteoporosis** → the **most important menopausal hazard**\n\n---\n\n## Putting it all together\n\nAnchor on: **combination therapy beats resistance**, **ovarian responds best / vagina not at all**, **GTD = methotrexate (low) or EMA-CO (higher)**, and the **drug-toxicity pairs** — cisplatin-kidney, adriamycin-heart, bleomycin-lung, methotrexate-liver, vincristine-nerves. These cover most of what is asked.",
         "tie": "These cues are the shortcut to answering chemotherapy questions, which cluster around drug-specific toxicities, which cancer responds to what, and the GTD regimens. Recognising the cue tells you the answer immediately."
